// serve — "make this app inspectable, if the launcher asked", per platform.
//
// ONE NAME an application calls, on any platform, so a shared entry does not
// have to know which toolkit it was compiled for. The resolver's platform
// override picks the real one: `serve_macos.cplus` on macOS and
// `serve_ios.cplus` on iOS, both siblings of this file.
//
// A target with neither lands HERE, and the honesty matters more than the
// convenience: an app that calls `arm` on a platform with no
// inspector backend still compiles and still runs, and simply is not
// inspectable. The alternative — resolving to some other platform's file —
// would be a link error at best and an inspector drawing into a window that
// does not exist at worst.
//
// The macOS implementation lives in `inspector/appkit`, which is also still
// importable by that name: it is the PLATFORM half (overlay, native rows, the
// UI-thread hop), where this is the entry an app calls. `serve_macos.cplus` is
// the seam between the two and is three lines long.

// Arm the inspector namespace on the agent's socket — the one line an
// application adds to make its live tree inspectable, on any platform.
//
// NOTHING TO ARM HERE. This target has no inspector backend, so the twelve
// `inspector.*` verbs have no implementation to expose and this is a no-op. The
// app still compiles, still runs, and still serves its AGENT surface if it
// asked for one; it simply is not inspectable. Resolving to some other
// platform's file instead would be a link error at best and an overlay drawing
// into a window that does not exist at worst.
fn arm() { return; }

